    <text><table><thead><tr><th>Problem</th><th>Clinical status</th><th>Verification</th><th>Code</th><th>Provenance</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  <tr ID="problem-1">
    <td>Headaches on waking for three weeks and blood pressure 184/102 mmHg at the visit (average of two seated readings with a large cuff); no chest pain, breathlessness, visual loss, confusion or weakness; fundi with arteriolar narrowing but no hemorrhages or papilledema; no hypertensive emergency; hypertension not yet confirmed</td><td>resolved</td><td>provisional</td>
    <td>R03.0</td><td>S — Synthetic presenting problem; resolved into stage 2 hypertension when a week of home readings confirmed it. Not a claim about her real health.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-2">
    <td>Stage 2 hypertension confirmed by home readings (seven-day average 168/96 mmHg), with left ventricular hypertrophy by ECG voltage; no albuminuria; 10-year cardiovascular risk about 14%; treated to reduce the risk of stroke, heart failure and kidney disease</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>I10</td><td>S — Synthetic modern diagnosis made at the second visit. It is linked to the recorded apoplexy before her death only as the reason for the stroke-prevention teaching; the record does not claim that she had hypertension.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-3">
    <td>Bilateral primary knee osteoarthritis, worse on the left: pain on stairs and on rising from a chair, morning stiffness under 30 minutes, crepitus and bony enlargement without warmth; clinical diagnosis without imaging</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>M17.0</td><td>S — Synthetic modern problem; not a claim about her real health.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-4">
    <td>Fall risk: one fall in the past year, a trip on the outside steps without injury; afraid of falling and going out less; STEADI screen positive; Timed Up and Go 15 seconds</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>Z91.81</td><td>S — Synthetic fall-risk problem.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-5">
    <td>Obesity, class 1 (BMI 31.6); she calls herself stout and wants to lose some weight for her knees</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>E66.811</td><td>S — Synthetic modern problem; her self-description as stout is documented, her weight is not.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-6">
    <td>Thiazide-associated hyponatremia (sodium 129 mmol/L) and hypokalemia (potassium 3.3 mmol/L) with orthostatic hypotension and a near-fall, after four days of a cold with little food while taking chlorthalidone; no confusion; resolved after chlorthalidone was stopped</td><td>resolved</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>E87.1</td><td>S — Synthetic adverse drug event at an unscheduled visit; a common effect of thiazide diuretics in older women, not a historical diagnosis.</td>
  </tr></tbody></table></text>

